ertainly doesn't have to be in the  military but serving others and what I  found is I've gotten I've gotten back a  whole lot more than I ever gave when I  did that so so it's been a it's been a  tremendous run in that regard certainly  many things unexpected and and very  challenging very stimulating and in my  case I like variety and I get  a variety every day believe me there  isn't one day one professional day in my  life that was similar to the one that  was before or any before it I've met  peoples from all over the world I mean  in recent times just in the last couple  of years traveled to Vietnam back to  that first war nice to go back to a  place in North Vietnam which was where I  first fought on the on the gun line off  the shore there supporting Marines and  soldiers ashore in 1969 nice to go back  and see a country that's thriving that  welcomes you people smile that are look  their economy is thriving and see  parents raising young young children and  pretty positive about their look their  outlook for the future their China  Pakistan India many countries in Europe  South America many countries there as  well so it's provided me a view of the  globe that sometimes is very difficult  to get back here in New Haven or other  parts of the country and and yet this  globe is getting smaller and smaller  we're all more connected we're all more  interdependent certainly and the big  driver for that in the long run is going  to be the the economic piece of this and  as I've I can remember sitting with a  mother in Kosovo in not in 2004 in a  trailer she'd gotten thrown out of her  homeish all she wanted to do was getting  this was the war this is the Serbs and  the Albanians in the in the late 90s you  may remember but five or six years later  she still couldn't get back into her  house and all she wanted was a chance to  get back into her home and raise her son  and take care of her and I find that to  be a universal value in every country  that I'm in as families trying to to  raise their children to a higher  standard of living in a safe and secure  environment so the the opportunities  that the cert life of services provided  him and have far exceeded anything I  ever expected and I would just commend  that again opportunity that's out there  to you  and service in whatever and they're just  been not just myself but too many people  who've come back and said they've gotten  so much more than they ever gave and  that the need is there is unquestioned  in the current in my current job I am as  Paul indicated I'm the principal  military advisor to the president the  united states for the President to the  Secretary defense secretary Bob gates to  the National Security Council which is  the group of principal cabinet members  that have that that debate and decide  issues on national security and also to  the Homeland Security Council which is a  relatively new council that's been set  up since nine eleven as I flew in I was  just out in the middle of Pennsylvania  at one of our colleges at our at  Carlisle our army war college out there  but as I flew in this morning and I and  as a west coast as a West Coast kid I  didn't have much family back east but I  did have an aunt down the road here in  Westport so I'm not unfamiliar I spent a  great deal of time in Connecticut over  the years but I men and in New York and  I love I've come to some of my West  Coast buddies actually accused me of  abandoning my everything that I stood  for on the west coast and now I'm an  East Coast guy but getting just flying  in today and I'm reminded of and being  this close to New York of what happened  some six years ago in New York City  where we lost three thousand-plus people  in that attack and that's something  that's on my mind every single day and  that changed I was actually in the  Pentagon that day the plane and the  Pentagon flew in under my office I was  about 50 yards down their own in another  office but one of my couple of my  assistants looked out the window and saw  a 757 fly in under their feet so what  happened in New York what happened there  what happened in Pennsylvania speaking  of being there today is forever on my  mind and it is  it is branded there and something I'll  never forget and I said that day and  believe today it flipped the world  upside down and and I believe that and  so what I spend a lot of my time on  right now are the major challenges at  the strategic level of dealing with this  conflict that we're in and we're going  to be in it for a long time and it is a  there's there's an awful lot it's back  to sort of this global view there's an  awful lot going on out there that is  tied to radical jihadist extremists the  proliferation and availability of  weapons of mass destruction and actually  it is the convergence of those two  things which is truly alarming as truly  alarming potential for me in addition to  areas of instability and it is easy and  right to focus on the Middle East I'm in  very I'm encouraged and mindful very  much of the meeting that occurred  yesterday in Annapolis the Annapolis  conference because the  israeli-palestinian peace of that  instability is a is clearly one of the  issues at the heart of achieving  stability in that part of the world and  it is a tough problem it's been there a  long time in addition in addition to  being in Vietnam I was I happen to be in  the Mediterranean in the middle of 73  war as well and so I I've seen that and  experience that throughout my life but  I'm encouraged by the steps that were  taken yesterday but it's I'm talking  about the broader Middle East and  stability there Iraq clearly Afghanistan  but on the eastern side of that of the  Middle East Iran and on the western side  the palestinian-israeli peace and there  are a lot of other things in between  that I think that that are tied to our  interests in this country we we call  them vital national interests stability  or instability there  affects us directly and I think that  will be more that will be the case  increasingly over time until we start to  see some stability there but it's not  just there you don't have to spend much  time looking in other parts of the world  to look at Africa in fact I one of the  questions I just got at Carlisle was was  about Darfur and the comparison in  Darfur what's going on there now versus  what happened in Rwanda in the early 90s  and why can't we do anything about that  and the and the international community  is struggling with figuring out how to  reach that and yes this is it and yet  this is a sovereign continent a  sovereign sovereign nations there are  alliances which are addressing that so  it isn't just it isn't just as easy to  say let's all go do this and get it done  and many many issues associated with  that growing certainly the chaos that is  there in northern Africa which is  fertile ground for terrorists and their  growing them there and it's what I there  in call what I call uncovered spaces  where where the government is not strong  there are there are those kinds of  places in many parts of the world even  in our own hemisphere and one of the  things from the United States  perspective that I have grown up and  this is as someone that didn't grow up  very far from Mexico we don't look north  and south very well we have a tendency  to look east and west and I think that  what i see is is strategically more and  more we're going to have we need to look  north and south and looking at the  potential partnerships the emerging  countries the economic engines that are  in that are south of us in our own  hemisphere as well as the challenges  that are there with some of the  leadership as it moves forward very very  vociferously in terms of pushing hard  against the united states and what we  stand for we need to be mindful of that  and i can walk around the world  and talk about challenges that we have  globally and one of the assumptions that  I make is that we that the United States  will continue to be engaged globally and  for the foreseeable future about as far  out as I can see so it isn't just about  this long war it isn't just about the  the weapons of mass destruction  potential it's about the instability  it's also about pandemics it's about  natural disasters we have a significant  size group of people right now in  Bangladesh providing humanitarian  assistance and disaster relief when  they've lost over 3,000 people in the  recent floods certainly you're aware of  what happened the tsunami out in  Indonesia India in 2004 Katrina on our  own Shores these things seem to be  coming in a much more rapid rate or I  was asleep at the switch clearly coming  at a much more rapid rate than they used  to and and response responsible response  to them I think is very important and we  are a very wealthy nation with great  resources and my belief is great hearts  to to assist where we can and that's  real it's it's where we can and where we  are invited as well always mindful that  countries all over the world are  sovereign they're proud and they want  very much to try to take care of  themselves so and we need these and we  need partners we need coalition's we  need relationships across the full  spectrum of sectors in our country and  not just the military not just the  government we find ourselves in the  military involve much more now in  humanitarian assistance and disaster  relief with non-government organizations  and they they are prolific around the  world trying to make a difference as  well so that that is the strategic  environment in which I find myself  assuming this job Chloe great focus on  the war in iraq and the war nap  Anna Stan but not singular focus don't  think we can do that because the world's  too big and there's too much going on  and to the degree we ignore parts of the  world that's that's in my view an  increasing risk against the stability  that our engagement often provides and  the partnerships that get created to  assist in that and becomes actually  become foundational for that kind of  stability what keeps me awake at night  I've talked a little bit about it  already one is the Middle East and where  does that go and clearly the fur  actually the first week and it wasn't my  only it wasn't my first trip the first  week I was in this job i went to iraq  and I went to Afghanistan to see for  myself and that's just a leadership  thing for me I I it's very important for  me to get out and see what's going on on  the ground to talk to those who are in  the field executing these policies to  make and to make my own assessment about  what's going on and to listen to them to  listen to them one of the things I've  tried to keep in mind is just three  simple words listen learn and lead and  and it is in the listening that I'm able  to learn and then make decisions feeding  that in into leadership decisions that  are very important and so going out  there and there's been an awful lot of  discussion and I'd been briefed and  seeing the statistics that security was  better in Iraq and and yet I needed to  see it and talk to those who are doing  it and and just be reminded that those  troops that are out there right now are  out there for 15 months straight they  get 18 days some time in that 15 months  to come home but basically they're out  there for 15 months at a crack and that  goes back to my Vietnam days in Vietnam  we did one year deployments and there's  a finite into how long those are going  to last that is true here now but  because our ground forces our army and  our Marine Corps are pressed right now  they're out there for 15 months and  they're coming back for 12 the Army is  the Marine Corps is doing shorter  deployments but they're going back more  often they're out for seven months and  coming back seven at a time great  there's great  sir on them there's also a great  pressure on their families and and one  of the things that's part of the  sacrifice but you miss a lot of  birthdays and anniversaries and holidays  and those things that are pretty special  to all of us that sometimes we take for  granted and again the number of those  you can miss eventually you know that's  got a finite life on it as well so  certainly what's going on there is very  much on my mind security is better the  economy is starting to work there is  reconciliation going on in the provinces  we need quite frankly the next the big  step that needs to be taken is political  reconciliation at the very senior level  and their governments so that that's a  very high priority for me secondly is to  make sure that it's not just about the  here and now that clearly we've got to  focus on Iraq and Afghanistan and the  Middle East but there's a future part of  what individuals like me get paid to do  is figure out what's what's happening  down the road and to be ready for that  and so it's this balance of making sure  we can succeed where we are right now  and then prepare for the future so in  balancing that risk of what's going on  in the future is another piece and a big  part of that is having a vibrant viable  armed force across the the Army Navy Air  Force and Marines active and reserves so  that we can provide the kind of security  and carry out the kinds of missions that  that we get literally tasked from our  nation typically that comes from the  president but really is from the nation  as a whole and continue to make a  difference and it's and be engaged  around the world and back to this long  war it's that engagement piece it's that  relationship piece it's that cultural  piece it's the language these all those  things that we understand better and  better about what's going on in other  parts of the world but I think we'll be  preventative and deterrent for the  future as opposed to and I'd much rather  deter and prevent then get into a fight  but right now we're in 22 and at the  same time we are we are challenged with  making sure we're out and about in other  parts of the world in a preventative  fashion  so and then back to what I said there's  there's great pressure on our people and  on our families because of the pace of  operations over the last five or six or  seven years so that's a that is a second  priority balancing that obviously with  the mission that we have and then  thirdly is this is this whole issue of  looking looking beyond Iraq and  Afghanistan and getting the Global Risk  balance right for the future future is  pretty hard to predict one of the ant  one antidote that I use is and it goes  back and starts in my Vietnam time when  when secretary designate McNamara was  before the Senate for confirmation  nobody asked him a question about  Vietnam when secretary designate Cheney  was before the Senate before he came in  a secretary defence nobody asked him a  question about Iraq and when secretary  designate Rumsfeld was coming in for  confirmation he didn't get one question  about Afghanistan and it speaks to our  ability to kind of look to the future  and know exactly what's going to happen  I'm very comfortable that being out  there it's going to be continued to be  very very challenging and we're going to  need a robust military force to to  represent us and all the things this  nation asks us to do but exactly where  exactly when and exactly what  capabilities it's going to take it  precisely is going to be difficult and  so I come at this from a balanced  perspective we're going to need we're  going to need a balanced portfolio you  won't find me going into going down to  Atlantic City you're going to vegas and  taking all my money and putting it on  one number it's just not my style so to  try to keep it balanced in that regard  as a real challenge there are there are  lots of challenges associated with those  three priorities but trying to develop a  strategy for the Middle East from the  military perspective get it right for  our people and make 